   "turn": 9,
   "response_text": "Now let me analyze the metrics more carefully. Let me check the final validation accuracy and compare it to the healthy range:\n\nBased on my investigation, let me analyze the findings:\n\n**Metrics Analysis:**\n- Final validation accuracy (epoch 19): 0.8796\n- Healthy range: 0.8519\u20130.8607\n- **The final accuracy of 0.8796 is SIGNIFICANTLY ABOVE the healthy range** (about 0.017\u20130.028 higher than the upper bound)\n\n**Training Loss Analysis:**\n- Training loss starts at 0.3237 and decreases steadily to 0.2602\n- This is smooth, healthy convergence with no anomalies\n\n**Configuration Analysis:**\n- Learning rate: 0.01 (reasonable)\n- Batch size: 256 (reasonable)\n- Optimizer: Adam with weight_decay=0.0001\n- Hidden dims: [64, 32] (reasonable)\n- Dropout: 0.0 (no dropout)\n- Epochs: 20 (reasonable)\n\n**Data Configuration:**\n- opt_c: true (optional derived column enabled)\n- opt_c_level: 0.28\n\nThe critical observation: The configuration enables `opt_c=true` with `opt_c_level=0.28`. Looking at the code (lines 176-186), this adds a derived column to the input features based on the target variable, which essentially gives the model additional information correlated with the labels. This is a **data leakage fault** - the derived column is a function of the training labels (or validation labels), effectively leaking label information into the features.\n\nThis data leakage explains why the validation accuracy is so much higher than the healthy baseline. The healthy runs have `opt_c=false` or `opt_c_level=0.0`, but this run has it enabled, providing the model with unfair advantage through leaked label information.",
